# PoN lifecycle reader and hosted native gate repair evidence

Base source: `da1374a200022bc14dc2bd325cc9ba29dbcd24ab`.
These captures describe uncommitted changes to the shared task checkout. They
do not certify a later commit, a complete native execution, or public consensus.

## Lifecycle evidence integrity

The unchanged reader accepted self-consistent material relabeling, replacement
of the frozen full-rank recipe, inversion of specialized-producer support, and
an arbitrary losing-proof digest. The new reader reuses the retained V2 material
recipes and constructor support matrix, and binds every trial digest to the
canonical proof prefix plus the reported trace. Full W1 replay and execution
authentication remain false.

- `before-fix-roster.*`: the final 15-method test code against the unchanged
  reader fails on 76 subtests: three material mutations, 72 specialized support
  inversions, and one losing-proof digest mutation.
- `after-fix.*`: the same 15 test methods pass with the repaired reader.
- `before-fix.*`: an earlier diagnostic test attempt is retained. Its 121 failed
  subtests include 45 generic support refusals whose error label differed from
  an over-specific assertion. Those 45 are not reader acceptance defects. The
  assertion was corrected before the `before-fix-roster` measurement.
- The original eleven test method names and their refusal assertions remain.
  The changed equal-winner fixture now uses a correctly bound synthetic digest
  before exercising its original cross-producer disagreement refusal.

The full fixed matrix has 117 producer/material pairs, with 59 supported pairs.
The eight samples and four searches per supported pair imply 1,888 searches;
exhausted searches are not counted as verified winners. These are roster
requirements, not a new native measurement or an all-algorithm lower bound.

## CI gate repair on original hosted logs

`replay_hosted_gates.py` extracts complete Rust test spans from the two retained
GitHub job logs by removing only each line's GitHub timestamp. The JSON reports
bind the original SHA-256, byte size, exact original line span, and derived
bytes. Original logs remain under the task's `observations` directory.

- Job `129678540672` actually has 13 passed tests, including the five shared
  observed-search controls. The old legal gate expected eight.
- Job `129678540829` actually has 11 factorial example passes and 33 History
  passes plus its one retained ignored subprocess fixture. The old gate expected
  six example passes and 28 History passes.
- `before-fix-hosted-gates.json`: both original inline gates reject their
  original successful test spans.
- `after-fix-hosted-gates.json`: both repaired inline gates accept exactly the
  same spans. This reinterprets the gate only; it does not change the recorded
  failed workflow conclusion or claim new Rust execution.
- `native-gate-before-fix.*`: nine gate test methods give three failures on the
  old workflow, including its acceptance of a stale eight-pass summary attached
  to the new 13-name roster.
- `native-gate-after-fix.*`: all nine methods pass, including missing, duplicate,
  replaced, failed, ignored, measured, filtered and duplicate-summary mutations.

The repaired legal and factorial gates require each complete current named
example/material/observer roster exactly once. Kernel named checks remain.
The original three History named checks remain, with the five additional
deadline controls explicitly required. No test set, ignored-fixture exception,
sampling target, attempt budget, or service deadline is reduced.

All actual W1/MU1 lower and upper cost points remain unknown. Scientific work
security, fresh model utility, independent demand, joint consensus security and
production activation remain outside these changes.

## Historical native V4 compatibility replay

The final compatibility check is now complete. See
`native-v4-historical-replay/README.md` and `replay-summary.json`.
The current stricter reader accepts the byte-identical retained complete native
journal (23,069 records) and preserves the original SIGTERM incomplete result.
Both readback JSON outputs are byte-identical to their historical outputs.
This is reader replay, not a new native execution; all cost/production
qualification flags remain false.
